Source: wiretap-server
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Alex Ferrara <alex@wiredsquare.com>
Homepage: https://github.com/Wired-Square/WireTAP-Server

# init-system-helpers is for deb-systemd-helper, which enables the unit where
# dpkg runs and systemd does not - an image-build chroot. adduser creates the
# system account the daemon runs as.
#
# can-utils is only suggested, not depended on: candump and cansend are what the
# troubleshooting documentation reaches for, but an ingest-only deployment has
# no local CAN hardware to point them at and should not be made to carry them.
#
# Architecture is `any` here and concrete in the built package; make-deb.sh
# overrides it per build.
Package: wiretap-server
Architecture: any
Depends: init-system-helpers (>= 1.54), adduser
Suggests: can-utils
Description: captures CAN bus traffic and archives it to a WireTAP gateway
 wiretap-server reads frames from a Linux host's SocketCAN interfaces, bridges
 them live to GVRET clients - the WireTAP desktop application and SavvyCAN -
 and forwards them to a WireTAP gateway for archiving. It also accepts frames
 pushed by microcontroller capture devices over a binary protocol, so a host
 with no CAN hardware of its own can still be the collection point for several
 that have.
 .
 A gateway outage does not cost frames. They spill to a local SQLite cache and
 drain in capture order when it comes back, ahead of whatever arrived in the
 meantime.
 .
 The binary is statically linked against musl, so this package declares no
 library dependencies and installs on any distribution with systemd.
